// SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0-only
//
// Expected: c1c1u0 — a blind signature scheme whose blinding factor is derived
// from the request identifier the client also publishes. The factor is not
// secret, so the blinding is not blinding: the signer recovers the message it
// was not supposed to see, and any observer links the blinded request to the
// signature that came back.
//
// blind_factor_leaked.vp makes the same point with an explicit `leaks`. This
// one is the version that occurs in practice, where nobody leaked anything and
// the factor was simply derived from something already public — which is
// harder to notice and exactly as fatal.
//
// The unlinkability verdict is the interesting one, and it HOLDS even though
// the scheme is thoroughly broken. That is not a bug and it is worth pinning.
// The link-witness procedure looks for a common secret origin by withholding
// each value from the attacker and asking what it would need to rebuild it;
// withhold the blinded term and the only route to the message disappears with
// it, so no shared ancestor is found. The decision procedure is sound for
// attacks and incomplete, u0 means "no witness was exhibited" rather than "the
// values are unlinkable", and this is the cheapest model in the tree that
// makes the difference visible.

attacker[passive]

principal Alice[
	knows public request_id
	knows private message
	factor = HASH(request_id)
	blinded = BLIND(factor, message)
]

Alice -> Signer: blinded

principal Signer[
	knows private sk
	gsk = PUBKEY(sk)
	blind_sig = SIGN(sk, blinded)
]

Signer -> Alice: [gsk], blind_sig

principal Alice[
	final_sig = UNBLIND(factor, message, blind_sig)
	leaks final_sig
]

queries[
	confidentiality? message
	confidentiality? factor
	unlinkability? blinded, final_sig
]
